**Proof.** Let $a_n = \dfrac{n}{\sqrt{1+|n|}}$ for $n\in\mathbb{Z}$. Note $a_0=0$ and the map $n\mapsto a_n$ is injective (since $x\mapsto x/\sqrt{1+|x|}$ is strictly increasing). By hypothesis $f(a_n)=0$ for every $n\in\mathbb{Z}$.

**Step 1 — Growth bound on $f$.** Integrating $f'$ along the segment from $0$ to $z$,
$$
f(z)=f(0)+\int_0^1 f'(tz)\,z\,dt,
$$
so, using $|f'(tz)|\le e^{|tz|}=e^{t|z|}$,
$$
|f(z)|\le |f(0)|+|z|\int_0^1 e^{t|z|}\,dt = |f(0)|+\bigl(e^{|z|}-1\bigr).
$$
Since $f(0)=f(a_0)=0$, we obtain $|f(z)|\le e^{|z|}-1\le e^{|z|}$. Hence $M(r):=\max_{|z|=r}|f(z)|\le e^{r}$, and in particular
$$
\log M(r)\le r. \tag{1}
$$

**Step 2 — Jensen's formula.** Let $m\ge 1$ be the order of the zero of $f$ at the origin. For every $r>0$ with $r\notin\{|a_n|:n\ne 0\}$, Jensen's formula gives
$$
\sum_{\substack{n\ne 0\\ |a_n|\le r}}\log\frac{r}{|a_n|}\;+\;m\log r
\;=\;\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\log\bigl|f(re^{i\theta})\bigr|\,d\theta
\;\le\; \log M(r)\;\le\; r. \tag{2}
$$
(The inequality holds because $\log|f(re^{i\theta})|\le \log M(r)$ pointwise on the circle — which contains no zeros by our choice of $r$ — and the left–hand side is nonnegative, so replacing $\log M(r)$ by $\log^+ M(r)\le r$ is valid.)

**Step 3 — Lower bound on the zero-counting function.** For $n\ge 1$,
$$
|a_n|=\frac{n}{\sqrt{1+n}}\le\frac{n}{\sqrt n}=\sqrt n.
$$
By symmetry $|a_{-n}|=|a_n|$. Hence for any $t\ge 2$, every integer $n$ with $1\le |n|\le t^2$ satisfies $|a_n|\le\sqrt{|n|}\le t$. Therefore the counting function
$$
N(t):=\#\{n\ne 0:|a_n|\le t\}\;\ge\;2\lfloor t^2\rfloor\;\ge\;t^2,\qquad t\ge 2. \tag{3}
$$

**Step 4 — Contradiction.** Using the standard identity $\displaystyle\sum_{|a_n|\le r}\log\frac{r}{|a_n|}=\int_0^r\frac{N(t)}{t}\,dt$ together with (3),
$$
\text{LHS of (2)}\;\ge\;\int_2^r\frac{N(t)}{t}\,dt+m\log r\;\ge\;\int_2^r\frac{t^2}{t}\,dt=\int_2^r t\,dt=\frac{r^2-4}{2}.
$$
Combining with (2) yields
$$
\frac{r^2-4}{2}\;\le\;r,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad r^2-2r-4\le 0,
$$
which is violated for all $r>1+\sqrt5$. This is impossible for arbitrarily large admissible $r$.

The contradiction forces $f\equiv 0$. $\blacksquare$

